# =============================================================================
# d32 — the decimal-width narrowing decline
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • The decimal conversio matrix admits exactly ONE widening — d32 → d64.
#     The reverse direction is a narrowing with no `↦` arm.
#   • The narrowing fails closed with the teaching diagnostic
#     `SEM016:decimal_width_narrowing_rejected` — the decimal width never
#     silently loses precision, symmetric with the tensor element law.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • writing `d64 ↦ d32` and expecting a runtime rounding step — the
#     narrowing is a type error, not a lossy conversion
#
# See also: ↦, numerus, d32, d64
# =============================================================================

# decimal — d64 → d32 narrowing reject
#
# WHY: the reverse of the widening row must reject at check time; there is
# no runtime path that narrows a decimal width.

fn narrow(numerus<d64> x) → numerus<d32> {
    return x ↦ numerus<d32>
}
